Two interfaces ask for the same method and a class implements both:
interface Named {
    String name();
}
interface Countable {
    String name();
}
public class Thing implements Named, Countable {
    ____________
}
Which single member belongs in the class so that new Thing().name() returns "thing"?

A class implements an interface without redeclaring anything, because its superclass already has a matching public method:
interface Pet {
    String speak();
}
class Animal {
    public String speak() {
        return "generic";
    }
}
class Dog extends Animal implements Pet {
}
Pet p = new Dog();
System.out.println(p.speak());
What does this program print?

A class implements two unrelated interfaces and an overload pair is called with an instance of it:
interface Left {
}
interface Right {
}
class Both implements Left, Right {
}
static void handle(Left x) {
    System.out.println("left");
}
static void handle(Right x) {
    System.out.println("right");
}
handle(new Both());
What happens when this is compiled and run?

Two interfaces declare a constant of the same name with different values and a class implements both:
interface Left {
    int SIZE = 1;
}
interface Right {
    int SIZE = 2;
}
public class Both implements Left, Right {
    int show() {
        return SIZE;
    }
}
What happens when this is compiled and run?

One class implements two interfaces and each is used through its own reference:
interface Flyable {
    String move();
}
interface Chargeable {
    String charge();
}
class Drone implements Flyable, Chargeable {
    public String move() {
        return "hover";
    }
    public String charge() {
        return "full";
    }
}
Flyable f = new Drone();
Chargeable ch = new Drone();
System.out.println(f.move() + " " + ch.charge());
What does this program print?

A class tries to satisfy an interface by declaring a method of the same name but a different type:
interface Report {
    String describe();
}
public class Doc implements Report {
    int describe() {
        return 1;
    }
}
What happens when this is compiled and run?

An interface pairs an abstract method with a default method that is written in terms of it:
interface Greet {
    String who();
    default String greet() {
        return "hi " + who();
    }
}
class Host implements Greet {
    public String who() {
        return "Ana";
    }
}
Greet g = new Host();
System.out.println(g.greet());
What does this program print?

Three interfaces form a chain, the middle one redeclaring the default, and a class implements only the last:
interface Alpha {
    default String who() {
        return "alpha";
    }
}
interface Beta extends Alpha {
    @Override
    default String who() {
        return "beta";
    }
}
interface Gamma extends Beta {
}
class Leaf implements Gamma {
}
System.out.println(new Leaf().who());
What does this program print?

A subinterface adds nothing, so the only default in the picture is the one at the top:
interface Alpha {
    default String who() {
        return "alpha";
    }
}
interface Beta extends Alpha {
}
class Leaf implements Beta {
}
System.out.println(new Leaf().who());
What does this program print?

A default method tries to consult a superclass method through super:
interface Alpha {
    default String who() {
        return super.toString().isEmpty() ? "alpha" : "alpha";
    }
}
class Leaf implements Alpha {
}
System.out.println(new Leaf().who());
What happens when this is compiled and run?

A class implements two unrelated interfaces that both supply a default who(), and the class wants the text of both:
interface Alpha {
    default String who() {
        return "alpha";
    }
}
interface Beta {
    default String who() {
        return "beta";
    }
}
public class Leaf implements Alpha, Beta {
    public String who() {
        ____________
    }
}
System.out.println(new Leaf().who());
Which single line belongs in the blank so that the program prints alpha/beta?

One interface supplies a default and an unrelated interface asks for the same method as an abstract declaration:
interface Alpha {
    default String who() {
        return "alpha";
    }
}
interface Beta {
    String who();
}
class Leaf implements Alpha, Beta {
}
System.out.println(new Leaf().who());
What happens when this is compiled and run?

A class resolves a default conflict by asking each interface for its own version and joining the results:
interface Alpha {
    default String who() {
        return "alpha";
    }
}
interface Beta {
    default String who() {
        return "beta";
    }
}
class Leaf implements Alpha, Beta {
    public String who() {
        return Alpha.super.who() + "+" + Beta.super.who();
    }
}
System.out.println(new Leaf().who());
What does this program print?

A class names both a parent interface and the subinterface that redeclares its default:
interface Alpha {
    default String who() {
        return "alpha";
    }
}
interface Beta extends Alpha {
    @Override
    default String who() {
        return "beta";
    }
}
class Leaf implements Alpha, Beta {
}
System.out.println(new Leaf().who());
