A subinterface must stop inheriting a parent's default and force every implementer to write its own value instead:
interface Loggable {
    default String tag() {
        return "log";
    }
}
interface Strict extends Loggable {
    ____________
}
Which single line belongs in the blank so that a class implementing Strict without writing tag() fails to compile?

One default calls a helper that a second, unrelated interface also supplies as a default:
interface Alpha {
    default String help() {
        return "a";
    }
    default String tag() {
        return help();
    }
}
interface Beta {
    default String help() {
        return "b";
    }
}
class Leaf implements Alpha, Beta {
}
System.out.println(new Leaf().tag());
What happens when this is compiled and run?

A class offers a read-only interface and a wider mutating class, and a caller tries to write through the narrow reference:
interface ReadonlyBox {
    int size();
}
class Box implements ReadonlyBox {
    private int cells;
    Box(int cells) {
        this.cells = cells;
    }
    public int size() {
        return cells;
    }
    public void setSize(int cells) {
        this.cells = cells;
    }
}
ReadonlyBox b = new Box(3);
b.setSize(5);
System.out.println(b.size());
What happens when this is compiled and run?

